## 2026-06-07 - US-012
- What was implemented: Added `BLURHASH_QUEUE:{ send:(message:unknown) => Promise<void> }` to `ArticleFetchConsumerEnv` in `src/server/article-fetch-consumer.ts`. Imported `extractImageUrls` from `./extract-image-urls.js`. Added `MAX_BODY_BLUR_IMAGES = 30` constant. After `writeFullContent`, when `'html' in result && result.html`, extracts image URLs, caps at 30, and sends one `{ imageUrl, itemId, objectId, target: 'body' }` job per image to `BLURHASH_QUEUE`. Removed the TODO placeholder comment. Extended `test/article-fetch-consumer.ts`: added `BLURHASH_QUEUE` with a `queued` capture array to `fakeEnv`, passed `queued` into all existing tests, added "enqueues body blur jobs on success" (2 image URLs → 2 queued jobs, fields asserted) and "does not enqueue blur jobs on failure" (0 queued). All 18 consumer tests pass.
- Files changed: src/server/article-fetch-consumer.ts, test/article-fetch-consumer.ts, specs/prd.json, progress.log
- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - Blur enqueue happens AFTER `writeFullContent` so a DO write failure (throw) skips enqueue — queue retry will re-fetch and re-enqueue, which is fine since blurhash jobs are idempotent via KV caching.
  - `'html' in result` is the correct TypeScript narrowing for the `FetchFullArticleResult` discriminated union — avoids needing to check `status === 'succeeded'` explicitly.
  - The node-platform fakeEnv pattern: capture queued messages in a `queued:unknown[]` array passed as the second argument; existing callers that don't care about blur can pass `[]`.
---

